From 1d504099db064bd575bff8b4d5140c8c6f3d3bdb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 6 <phase6@openwch.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 18:40:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] riscv: accept the WCH QingKe 'WCH-Interrupt-fast'
 interrupt value

The stock backend rejects the interrupt value every WCH EVT interrupt
handler uses, warns "unrecognized argument", and emits an ordinary function.
Three objects of the quick gate still differ from the WCH build for exactly
that reason -- the official object leaves the handler through mret where
ours leaves through ret:

  tmp/golden/8.2.0/v3b-pioc/run1/obj/0000_RGB1W.c.o          (mret at 0x16)
  tmp/golden/8.2.0/v4bc-pmp/run1/obj/0000_ch32v20x_it.c.o
  tmp/golden/8.2.0/v4bc-pmp/run1/obj/0001_main.c.o

The QingKe core pushes and pops the caller-saved GPRs and the return address
in hardware when such a handler is entered, so the software prologue must
leave that register set alone while keeping the ordinary interrupt treatment
for everything else.

Spec source, all first-hand measurements of the official binary
ref/gcc/darwin-arm64/8.2.0/bin/riscv-none-embed-gcc.

Archived matrix, tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/07-c-attributes:
i1 interrupt and i2 interrupt("machine") save the temporaries and return by
mret; i3 interrupt("WCH-Interrupt-fast") saves nothing and still returns by
mret; i5 uret and i6 sret are unchanged; i4 "WCH-Interrupt-Fast" and i7
"wch-interrupt-fast" keep the upstream warning and degrade to ret, so only
the exact spelling is accepted.  i3-noxw shows the value is not gated on the
xw extension: the .o is byte-identical under -march=rv32imac.

Directed matrix, tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d4/spec-probe/official,
which fixes what the archived cases leave open.  p01, a non-leaf handler,
saves neither ra nor any temporary, so the hardware-saved set has to include
the return address even though GCC's own table calls it call-saved.  p02, a
leaf handler using floats, still spills fa3/fa4/fa5 while saving no GPR, and
p03 spills every call-used FPR -- the FPRs keep the ordinary interrupt rule,
which is why this is a subtraction from riscv_save_reg_p rather than a switch
to ordinary function frames.  p04 and p12 keep the callee-saved GPRs
software-saved.  p05 uses t1 as the large-frame temporary without saving it,
which is why the interrupt_save_t1 computation is skipped.  p06 shows shrink
wrapping gives such a handler an exit that bypasses the interrupt epilogue.
p09 and p11 keep the upstream naked and return-value errors.

Second matrix, tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d4/spec-probe/extra-official,
which fixes the model.  The value neither sets nor conflicts with a privilege
mode: m01 interrupt("user","machine") still errors, while m02 and m03 pair it
with user and supervisor and are accepted, keeping uret and sret in the
epilogue; m04 tolerates it twice.  It is therefore a flag beside the two
SiFive CLIC flags, not a fourth privilege level.  sw03 and sw04 show the
shrink-wrapped exit stays mret even when the epilogue returns by uret or
sret, so riscv_output_return is not mode-dispatched.  m05 combines it with
SiFive-CLIC-preemptible and gets a bare fast frame with no CSR traffic,
which needs no extra guard: the preemptible prologue already sits inside the
"any register saved" branch of riscv_expand_prologue, and a fast handler
leaves that mask empty.

8 new tests in gcc.target/riscv, the one gcc face with a pristine baseline
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/testsuite-pristine): 30 new PASS tuples, no
baseline tuple removed.
